{#
  Bilingual text helpers.

  A page that declares `docLang` (posts, project pages, the legal pages, and the
  /de/ twins of the homepage, blog and certificates) is written in one language,
  so only that variant is rendered. Google decides a page's language from its
  visible text, and every hidden translation would only blur it.

  Pages without `docLang` (tag pages, 404) still ship both variants;
  `html[data-lang]` plus the rules in style.css decide which one is visible.
  Each variant carries its own `lang` for screen readers and Pagefind.

  Usage:  {% import "partials/i18n.njk" as i18n with context %}
          {{ i18n.t("Certificates", "Zertifikate") }}

  `with context` is what lets the macros see the page's `docLang`; without it
  they always render both variants.

  `de` may be empty — the English string is then used for both languages, which
  is what the CMS-managed data files rely on for untranslated fields.

  Text is escaped and its newlines become <br>, so multi-paragraph CMS fields
  render as written instead of collapsing into one run-on block.
#}

{% macro t(en, de) %}{% if docLang == "de" %}{{ (de if de else en) | nl2br | safe }}{% elif docLang == "en" %}{{ en | nl2br | safe }}{% else %}<span data-lang-en lang="en">{{ en | nl2br | safe }}</span><span data-lang-de lang="de">{{ (de if de else en) | nl2br | safe }}</span>{% endif %}{% endmacro %}

{#- Same, but for pre-rendered HTML (e.g. markdown output). -#}
{% macro tHtml(en, de) %}{% if docLang == "de" %}{{ (de if de else en) | safe }}{% elif docLang == "en" %}{{ en | safe }}{% else %}<span data-lang-en lang="en">{{ en | safe }}</span><span data-lang-de lang="de">{{ (de if de else en) | safe }}</span>{% endif %}{% endmacro %}
